WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.700
- [Mark] Hello Everyone, and welcome

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.290
back to The After Show.
My name is Mark Ellis

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.990
and one summer in
college I painted houses,

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.900
not like they do in The Irishman.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.140
That's what we're talking about today.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.280
If you have not yet seen
the film The Irishman,

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.750
be warned the spoilers will follow.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:17.366
This movie is currently 99% fresh

00:00:17.366 --> 00:00:20.630
on the Tomatometer, with the critics.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:22.830
98 critics are weighing in so far,

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.200
that's bound to increase.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.070
We'll see how the Tomatometer responds.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.860
So excited to talk about this movie.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.630
Martin Scorsese's latest,
is it his greatest?

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.350
I certainly have two great panelists

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.183
here today.

00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:34.775
Angie Han is joining us.

00:00:34.775 --> 00:00:35.608
- Hi.

00:00:35.608 --> 00:00:36.441
- [Man] Hi Angie.

00:00:36.441 --> 00:00:38.348
- [Mark] And Scott Mantz the Irish 'Mantz'.

00:00:38.348 --> 00:00:40.580
- The Irishman, yes. That's the sequel.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.220
- [Mark] That's the
sequel to The Irishman,

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.253
and it's probably going
to be an even longer film.

00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:45.086
- Yes

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:47.890
- The critics consensus
right now on Rotten Tomatoes,

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.850
reads as "An epic
gangster drama that earns

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.320
its extended runtime. The
Irishman finds Martin Scorsese

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.190
revisiting familiar themes to poignant,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.440
funny, and profound effect."

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.680
So before we get really
into this conversation,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.880
I just really want to get y'alls take.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.300
Is this movie fresh or rotten?

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.810
Scott, do you agree with
what is on the Tomatometer?

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.570
- Absolutely. I think that it's

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.990
up there for a reason.
You know, Martin Scorsese,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.500
this is his 26th feature film.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.870
Not his first mob movie.

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.730
One of his best, is it his greatest?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:17.210
We'll find out during this conversation.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.170
I won't spoil it at the top.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.990
But I think it is absolutely worthy

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:20.823
of all the raves.

00:01:20.823 --> 00:01:22.260
It lives up to the hype.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.476
- [Mark] It is definitely
not his first crime movie.

00:01:24.476 --> 00:01:25.360
(laughing)

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.940
Angie, do you agree with Scott?

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.770
Is this a fresh movie in your eyes?

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.350
- For me yes, and its also one that,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:32.450
as I was watching it, I
started out liking it,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.930
and I feel like I liked it
more and more as I was going,

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.040
so I gotta be honest, at
first I was just like,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.537
I dont't know, people were
very high on this movie,

00:01:39.537 --> 00:01:41.710
and I was like okay, it's pretty good.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.520
And then, by the end I was just like yes!

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.910
I'm up there with everyone else.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.870
- It's always one of those
things where you go into a movie,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.200
and you'd heard a little bit
of hype from people who got to

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.350
see advance screenings,
and you're like okay well,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.230
first of all do I see
this movie in theaters,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.180
or do I wait until it's on Netflix?

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.810
It's got a three and a half hour run time.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.500
I went in. I saw this movie
and I think that I continue

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.180
to fall more in love with it,
even after I left the theater.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.310
Because it is such a long movie and you

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.050
feel the length, in my opinion.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.340
But it doesn't necessarily mean it's

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.665
a detriment to the movie,

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:13.210
there isn't a scene I'd lose.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.810
- Yeah, I feel like you feel the length,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.800
but in a good way because I
feel like what works for it,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.450
the length works for
it because by the end,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.220
you really want to feel the weight

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.510
of everything that has happened,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.850
and that wouldn't work if it has been

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.850
like a 90 minute movie.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.480
- Well, you know what you
said about how you know

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.057
you went in and initially you were like

00:02:29.057 --> 00:02:31.470
"Oh, I don't know" but then it
got better as it went along.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.231
I absolutely feel that that's
the strength of this movie,

00:02:34.231 --> 00:02:35.379
is that it does get
better as it goes along.

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:39.200
And I also think the
running time is justified,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.610
because unlike Goodfellas
or Casino, which are very,

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:45.470
very fast paced, quick cut movies,

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.250
this movie is a little more restrained

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.450
compared to those films.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.440
You don't have those quick cuts,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.210
the scenes are allowed to breathe,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.110
the performances are allowed to breathe,

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.960
so you need more time to get into it,

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.190
for the movie to sort of
cast its spell over you,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:05.050
but it also goes places,
especially towards the end

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.400
in the last hour,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.280
where none of the Scorsese's
mob movies have gone,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.840
and the performances in
this movie are off the hook.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.480
- Well that's why I was happy that I timed

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.430
my bathroom break correctly.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.380
If you haven't seen the movie,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.470
it was about two and a half hours in,

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.890
because like I got back and I sat down,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.310
and I might have gotten some more popcorn,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.880
and then I knew there's a certain twist

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.427
that happens with the film and you're like

00:03:26.427 --> 00:03:30.710
"Oh God, okay wait De Niro
is gonna have to take care

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.390
of Pacino's character
himself, the Irishman is gonna

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.270
have to take care of Jimmy Hoffa himself,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.890
and I was so glad that I
could just enjoy that last

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.301
45 minutes hour of the movie
and all the other surprises

00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:42.370
that are in there because
Scott I think you hit

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.200
on something that this
is a familiar tone with

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.870
what Martin Scorsese has done in the past,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.660
obviously with like Goodfellas and Casino,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.320
but the filmmaking is different,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.340
and the tone is a little different too.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.960
This isn't quite as violent,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.750
its a little bit more of a tender take.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.107
- Well tender for a mob film, okay. But --

00:03:59.107 --> 00:04:01.450
- There are still plenty
of houses being painted.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:06.290
- But you feel sort of
empathy for Robert De Niro

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.530
as Frank Sheeran, the
Irishman of the title.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.800
So, look at a filmmaker
like Clint Eastwood.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.320
He made a bunch of Westerns,

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.750
and then he waits until
just the right time

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.560
to make one of the greatest
Westerns of all time,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.620
his best Western, which was Unforgiven.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.390
That was his coda, that
was his career capper

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.270
for the westerns.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:26.850
It doesn't get better than that.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.110
And I feel like that's
sorta the tone that Scorsese

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.700
is taking with the Irishman.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.550
- [Angie] Yeah, it makes for
a really interesting companion

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.480
with his other gangster
movies because I feel like

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:35.313
one of the big differences,

00:04:35.313 --> 00:04:37.530
you know you watch Goodfellas,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.990
and obviously you also see the dark side

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:42.020
of it and there's the whole
downfall and everything.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.510
So it's not like everything is great,

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.560
but it's such a movie about the appeal and

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.940
the allure and the glamor
and the excitement of

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:51.050
this world,

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.410
whereas I feel like this
movie doesn't really do that,

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.900
it doesn't really look...

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.330
even as you're entering
this world with them,

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.150
it doesn't really look that sexy,

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.900
it just kinda looks like,
you know it's his job.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:01.760
He's working.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.930
- Is it almost too early
to try to rank this movie

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.650
against his other crime films,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.750
because you know I think
we're such a prisoner

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:11.490
in the moment sometimes,
where I just saw The Irishman

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.500
and oh my God it's such
a different way for

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.340
some of these performers,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.713
like it was a totally
different Pesci than we got

00:05:16.713 --> 00:05:17.546
in Casino and Goodfellas,

00:05:17.546 --> 00:05:21.180
but as I'm thinking about it today,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.040
I'm thinking The Irishman
might be just as good

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.550
of a film as Goodfellas and Casino,

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.860
I just don't know if it's as
re-watchable as those two.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.280
- [Scott] Well okay well,
I think that The Irishman

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:32.900
is definitely re-watchable to the extent

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:34.590
I can't wait to watch it again,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:35.730
and here's the beauty of it.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.430
You can see it in theaters
during the three week run

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.230
it has before it does drop on Netflix.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.063
- Are you going to see it in the theater,

00:05:41.063 --> 00:05:42.904
would you send people to
the theater to see it,

00:05:42.904 --> 00:05:44.403
or would you tell them
to wait for Netflix?

00:05:44.403 --> 00:05:46.090
- I would absolutely say,
you've gotta see this movie

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:46.923
on the big screen.

00:05:46.923 --> 00:05:48.550
Forget about the running
time, just let yourself

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.120
get consumed by the film.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.440
It is absolutely worthy.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.100
This is a milestone, a
landmark cinematic achievement

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.573
and that always does need to
be seen on the big screen.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:04.420
But, there's something
about the comfort of knowing

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.950
that a movie is three
and a half hours long and

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.630
watching it at home, and
you go okay I'm gonna

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.320
pause it here I'll watch
the rest of it tomorrow,

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.650
and you know what you can
watch it over and over again --

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.250
- [Angie] In terms of ranking them,

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:17.860
I find it really difficult
because I like them

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.420
for such different reasons,
they're doing such different

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:21.780
things, they're such different films.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.460
I don't know, to me they
work so well together,

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.645
I'm like I don't know
why I'd put them against

00:06:26.645 --> 00:06:27.478
each other --

00:06:27.478 --> 00:06:28.350
- Not putting them against each other --

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.110
(all speaking simultaneously)

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:33.810
- I'm trying to compare
which one's better,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.020
and I'm like I don't know,
to me part of the fun is

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.030
that they work so well
together as bookends

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.250
of this kind of career that he's had,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.110
not that this is his last
movie or that Goodfellas

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.330
was his first movie, but you know,

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.380
he was much younger when he made that one,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.530
and he's much older now,

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:48.830
but then as to your other question

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.360
about whether I'd tell
people to go see it,

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:53.240
I think yes. I don't
necessarily know that it's gonna

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.770
be something that you could
just pop on at midnight

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:59.008
and suddenly watch all three
hours of, you know whatever,

00:06:59.008 --> 00:07:01.830
but I do think that part
of the reason I would

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.390
advise anyone who's
interested in this movie

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.590
to go see it in theaters
is because I think it's

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.230
a film that requires some patience and

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.560
I don't know about you but
like I find it sometimes

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.510
very difficult even when
I'm enjoying a movie

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:14.220
to watch a movie that requires
a lot of patience at home

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.720
because there's just so
many distractions around,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.050
like I was really glad
that, especially because

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.040
I think the first hour
is a little bit slower,

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.270
I was really glad that I was
watching it in a situation

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.873
where there was no escape.

00:07:23.873 --> 00:07:25.920
- Right, it's a compliment
to Dolemite Is My Name

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.670
that I didn't pick up my
phone once watching that

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.390
from home, because that's where I was.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:32.480
It hard. In the modern world
we live in its very tough

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.420
to focus on anything for
three minutes much less

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:36.300
three and a half hours,

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.840
so I would tend to recommend
see it in the theater

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:39.790
if you have the means.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.070
And that's also because I
think the theater experience

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:46.120
is gonna make the de-aging
look a little cleaner.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.630
I've noticed that with
de-aging, like I was watching

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.530
Rogue One the other day
at home, and it's like

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.360
they did a different thing
with Grand Moff Tarkin

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:54.230
there than what Scorsese
did with the de-aging,

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:57.300
but it's still, it comes
across more as an effect

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:59.160
on the small screen to me anyway,

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.410
because the de-aging in
this did not take me out of

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.329
the story at all, and I was thrilled--

00:08:03.329 --> 00:08:04.920
- [Angie] It did to me, a little bit.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.576
- [Scott] It did a little
bit in the beginning.

00:08:06.576 --> 00:08:10.230
Like it's obviously first
noticeable when you see

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:14.090
the younger versions of
Scorsese and Al Pacino

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:16.860
and Frank Sheeran played
by De Niro and Joe Pesci's

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.020
character, it's definitely,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.100
you're alerted to it,
it's noticeable, but --

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.133
- It's just the eyes.

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:25.370
There's like a glassy eyes,
but again I don't know

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.630
if they're just making his eyes glassy for

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:30.016
different reasons cause
that's how the guy looked.

00:08:30.016 --> 00:08:30.849
(laughing)

00:08:30.849 --> 00:08:31.682
Like I don't know.

00:08:31.682 --> 00:08:32.710
- But I also felt like it was
a little bit of the skin too.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:35.905
I was looking at the
whole package watching,

00:08:35.905 --> 00:08:37.350
I was like, okay this
is the de-aging process

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.600
they were talking about, but
the narrative of the film

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.620
did take over, the characters took over.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:46.210
And here's why I think,
ultimately the de-aging process

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:51.040
was more crucial, rather
than casting younger actors

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.710
to play the younger versions
of these characters,

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:57.030
you're with these images
from start to finish,

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.522
three and a half hours, like decades,

00:08:59.522 --> 00:09:03.540
and it makes you drawn
to the characters more,

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.010
you're more vested,
emotionally, to the characters

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:09.630
and the stakes and the epic-ness of it all

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.980
when you're seeing one person
play the same character

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.130
over the course of,
whatever, five decades.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.540
- [Mark] I think
especially when that person

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.389
is so recognizable in a role like this,

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:20.980
where you have De Niro,
Pacino, Pesci, Harvey Keitel,

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:25.100
it's a great nugget from
Charlotte O'Sullivan,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.440
she said "If this something
of a tall story, though,

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.746
it's an irresistible one,
with Pesci and Harvey Keitel

00:09:28.746 --> 00:09:30.970
especially sharp."

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.820
So it's like this is not just
a movie for the headliners,

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.870
for oh De Niro and Pacino
are in a movie together,

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:38.320
like I thought Pesci
stole the entire movie,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.043
I think De Niro could
get nominated but Angie,

00:09:40.043 --> 00:09:43.650
I think Joe Pesci playing
against type, from what we saw

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:45.760
him in Goodfellas and Casino,

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:48.000
I think this was a landmark
achievement for him.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.213
- [Angie] I thought he
was incredible in this,

00:09:51.134 --> 00:09:53.960
not that you'd expect
anything less than greatness

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.280
from any of these
people, but like you said

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:58.450
it's so different from what
we've seen him do before,

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:01.270
and I feel like that really
adds the experience too,

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:03.900
where I kind of felt myself projecting

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:06.460
his kind of intensity
under this character,

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:08.630
and even when he's restrained
you feel like there's

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.810
something in him waiting to
just jump out (laughing).

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.140
- [Mark] And I don't know
if that was just because

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:13.531
I know Joe Pesci movies,
but I was waiting for him

00:10:13.531 --> 00:10:15.953
to turn into Leo Getz from Lethal Weapon.

00:10:15.953 --> 00:10:16.882
(laughing)

00:10:16.882 --> 00:10:19.000
Just explode everybody,
but he's just so quiet,

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.130
and so reserved the whole
time, and it makes for

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:22.540
such a powerful dynamic.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.470
- You know, Joe Pesci
is great in this film,

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:26.986
and he's completely different,
like the way we've seen

00:10:26.986 --> 00:10:31.050
him in Goodfellas and Casino
and Lethal Weapon 2, buddy.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:35.490
But it is really for me,
great to see Pesci making

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:37.360
his first movie in nine years.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:40.073
He came out of retirement,
so to speak, to do this film.

00:10:40.073 --> 00:10:44.250
It was definitely worthy, but
I think the standout here,

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:47.253
while there are two, really
one of them is De Niro.

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:51.620
Robert De Niro, especially in
the last hour of this movie,

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:54.890
is as good as he has ever been.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.870
He has to be nominated and
I think he's a front runner

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.070
to actually win.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:00.000
- That's a hot take.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.480
Angie, you think De Niro
is the front runner in

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.416
the best lead performance category?

00:11:04.416 --> 00:11:06.825
- I don't know because it's just so early,

00:11:06.825 --> 00:11:08.370
I can definitely see him being

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.310
a really strong contender,
but I agree with you.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:13.320
It's a movie where all
three of these leading men

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.550
have been really just
thought of as icons forever,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:19.650
and they've had towering reputations,

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:21.320
and I do feel like sometimes when

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.559
an actor gets to that
level it becomes easy

00:11:22.559 --> 00:11:25.940
to stop appreciating what they're doing,

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.590
it becomes easy to take them for granted.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.860
It becomes easy to kind
of like, you know be like

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:32.490
oh yeah sure, I'm sure
he's great whatever,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.520
but then this is one of
those movies where you see

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.250
all three of them and
you're just like oh right,

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:39.534
this is such a vital reminder
of why we worship them so much

00:11:39.534 --> 00:11:44.057
in the first place and this is
why they're still so exciting

00:11:44.057 --> 00:11:45.510
and why it's still
thrilling to go see them

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:46.560
on the big screen.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.140
- [Scott] One of the
best scenes of the movie,

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:51.440
it's not what you think,
its not a whacking scene,

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.230
which by the way, another
thing that separates this

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:56.810
from other mob movies,
especially Scorsese movies,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:58.106
no one uses the word whacked,

00:11:58.106 --> 00:12:00.580
no one uses the word 'forgetaboutit'.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:02.120
It doesn't fall prey to those cliches --

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.701
- We're just painting houses,
that's all we're doing.

00:12:04.701 --> 00:12:05.608
- Yeah we're just painting houses.

00:12:05.608 --> 00:12:08.460
But the scene, okay so this
is a spoiler heavy show,

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.420
so we see Frank Sheeran
knock off Jimmy Hoffa,

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:14.400
played by Al Pacino, we'll
get to Pacino in a second,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.280
but the scene when Frank
Sheeran, De Niro, has to call

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.910
Jimmy Hoffa's wife and offer
his condolences and say

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.170
I'm really sorry, don't worry
you know I'm here for you

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.060
if you need me, I'll talk to you tomorrow.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:30.540
Like an expert, a master craftsman,

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.810
a master class in acting right there.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.690
He knows what he did, his
daughter knows what he did,

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.362
okay Anna Paquin, and he has
to pretend like he's there

00:12:39.362 --> 00:12:41.220
for his wife.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.080
That moment is just going,
this guy is a freaking genius.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:46.110
- [Mark] I'm glad you brought
up the Anna Paquin scene,

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.277
because Anna Paquin, I thought, kind of a,

00:12:48.277 --> 00:12:51.340
she definitely doesn't
have a lot of lines.

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.770
But such a powerful haunting performance,

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:56.410
just as the daughter
who knows exactly what

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.470
her dad is into and we get
that matriculation of when

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:02.820
she's a little girl and
seeing him and how firm

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:05.679
of a defender he is, but also
you grow up and you get older

00:13:05.679 --> 00:13:08.090
and I saw that there was
an interesting article,

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:09.152
there's been a few of them this week,

00:13:09.152 --> 00:13:13.230
about Scorsese and the fact
that women are by and large

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.467
not involved in this storyline
at all, they're silent

00:13:15.467 --> 00:13:18.330
and even at the beginning of the movie,

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:19.900
it's almost like they're a
nuisance cause you have to

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.300
pull over so they can smoke.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.290
So how did you all feel about the lack of

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.500
a strong female role in this?

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.930
Or do you think Anna Paquin fit that bill?

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:30.504
- I don't think Anna Paquin fits the...

00:13:30.504 --> 00:13:34.580
I wouldn't say she's this
powerful female character,

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:37.040
because she is very much a
character whose whole role

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:38.820
is being on the sidelines,
watching what's going on,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.300
she has very few lines,
she doesn't actually

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:41.677
do that much or drive
that much of the action

00:13:41.677 --> 00:13:43.650
or anything like that.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:47.410
That said, I think to me it
made sense that the women

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:50.460
were so secondary in this movie
just because it seems like

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.580
that's the role that
they fill in the lives of

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.000
these men that the
movie is actually about.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.140
It doesn't seem like Robert De
Niro particularly understands

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:01.120
his daughter that well.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.290
It seems like his character
sees her as more of a symbol,

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:06.610
so it makes sense that the
movie about him would also

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.470
see her that way, and --

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.100
- Yeah, I think that Anna
Paquin's performance was actually

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:11.450
very, very, very strong.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:13.130
I mean, that's what acting is.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:14.860
You know, show us don't tell us.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:18.335
And for the duration of when
the daughter is her age,

00:14:18.335 --> 00:14:20.500
and Anna Paquin is playing her,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.980
we see what she's feeling, we're not told.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:26.430
And I think that makes for
a more powerful performance.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:27.450
And I do agree with you.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.540
I think if the movie took place today,

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.330
if it was a mob movie about today,

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:33.670
I think it would have
been much, much different.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:36.170
Yes, the women should
have had much stronger,

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:37.390
more prominent roles.

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:40.010
- Women existed in the 1950s
and they were doing things,

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:42.321
so I don't necessarily
know that it's fair to say

00:14:42.321 --> 00:14:44.690
women weren't really
around and doing anything

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.140
so movies set in the
1950s wouldn't have them,

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:51.280
but I do think in this movie,
that's not how he saw his wife

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:53.760
that's not how the other
men saw their wives

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:55.310
and other women in their lives.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:04.150
I'm not saying let's have
no strong female characters

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.150
in any movies or anything,

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.570
but I'm saying you gotta
look at it for what works

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:10.651
for the specific movie and
then this one, I was like okay.

00:15:10.651 --> 00:15:12.395
It makes sense to me.

00:15:12.395 --> 00:15:13.228
- [Mark] Yeah, I agree. --
- [Scott] So if you look at...

00:15:13.228 --> 00:15:15.687
Well, listen. Let's talk
about Pacino in this movie.

00:15:15.687 --> 00:15:16.520
- Yeah.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:18.370
- Because you know what. First of all,

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.420
Al Pacino in a movie
directed by Martin Scorsese,

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.250
are you kidding, this has never happened?

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.270
This is Al Pacino's first
movie with Martin Scorsese

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.181
after working with all these
amazing directors, I mean,

00:15:30.181 --> 00:15:33.780
and only now in 2019 are we
finally seeing this happen.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:36.580
First of all perfect fit, perfect fit.

00:15:36.580 --> 00:15:40.600
- [Angie] But seeing when he's,
when Robert is at his dinner

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:43.620
and he's trying to explain
to Jimmy Hoffa what's gonna

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.820
happen and Jimmy Hoffa
is just not getting it,

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:48.660
I wanted to just crawl
under a blanket and die,

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:51.440
it's just so tense and so heartbreaking.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.320
- Yeah you think about all
the scenes in this movie

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.760
and what it adds up to
is, again you're seeing

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:58.320
a lot of critics mention the run time,

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.170
but not necessarily that it's a detriment,

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:02.060
so naturally when its talk of,

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:03.510
could it be a best picture nominee,

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:04.550
could it win best picture,

00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:06.710
and what does this mean for Netflix,

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.300
coming off of the win with Roma last year,

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:10.950
making them a bigger player in the game.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:13.410
- Okay the Roma win for last year was for

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:16.000
foreign film, director and cinematography.

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:19.930
Now, last year Netflix got
behind Roma obviously because

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.300
we all got those Roma pillows.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.150
But this year, especially,
2019 is really the year

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:29.460
that Netflix came to
the party and took over

00:16:30.557 --> 00:16:31.430
and took the stage.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:32.750
Not only do they have The Irishman,

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:35.010
they have Dolemite Is My Name,
they have Marriage Story,

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:36.260
they have The Two Popes,

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:40.650
like they are doing real,
actual quality driven,

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:43.510
cinematic features that
are playing in theaters

00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:46.460
before they eventually drop on Netflix.

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:48.630
But the other thing with The Irishman is,

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:50.580
is it a front runner to win?

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:54.219
Possibly, because when Martin
Scorsese won best picture

00:16:54.219 --> 00:16:56.310
and director for The Departed.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:57.600
I like The Departed.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:01.147
But back in 2006, that movie
opened October 6th, 2006.

00:17:01.147 --> 00:17:02.579
- [Mark] Good man!
- [Scott] When The Departed

00:17:02.579 --> 00:17:06.040
came out, I remember
when I saw it I thought,

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:09.630
really good film, but not
one of Scorsese's best,

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:11.687
and I don't really think it's
really an Oscar contender.

00:17:11.687 --> 00:17:13.700
Boy, was I wrong.

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:15.410
But was it his best picture?

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:17.070
I mean, was it better than Taxi Driver,

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:18.488
was it better than
Goodfellas, was it better than

00:17:18.488 --> 00:17:20.660
Raging Bull. No, no, no.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:23.138
That movie won director
and picture because --

00:17:23.138 --> 00:17:26.119
- It was seen as the
Lifetime Achievement Award.

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:27.713
- It was, we gotta give
it to him sometimes

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:29.107
so here's an opportunity.

00:17:29.107 --> 00:17:29.940
- Right but guess what?

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:34.940
Now he's making a movie that
is as good as those films.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.220
And you're going like, this
is the movie that Scorsese

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:43.220
actually should have won the
Academy Award for director

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:46.110
and best picture for, and
maybe he will because I think

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:48.440
people will be like,
this is an amazing film.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.454
- [Mark] All right, so
quickly. Let's talk about

00:17:50.454 --> 00:17:52.150
the movies that The Irishman
might be going up against

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:53.260
that are not Netflix films.

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:56.670
What are some of your other
best picture contenders?

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:57.710
- Parasite --

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:58.850
- Parasite's a big one.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:17:59.683
Farewell.

00:18:00.561 --> 00:18:01.394
- [Angie] I feel like
I've heard people guessing

00:18:01.394 --> 00:18:02.430
Jojo Rabbit, as well.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:04.450
- [Scott] Jojo Rabbit,
that did win the top prize

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:06.386
in Toronto, and that is a good (mumbles).

00:18:06.386 --> 00:18:08.910
I think that Jojo Rabbit
will definitely get nominated

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.177
for best picture --

00:18:10.177 --> 00:18:11.010
- Didn't Green Book win
that prize last year?

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:11.843
- Yes, it did. It did.
And it won Best Picture.

00:18:11.843 --> 00:18:15.357
- Right, so I think Jojo
Rabbit is one of those,

00:18:15.357 --> 00:18:17.598
I put that with The Farewell
too where it's like,

00:18:17.598 --> 00:18:20.750
because we have 10 nominees,
and then The Farewell

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.400
came out a little bit ago,
but I think that you could

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.976
definitely have a campaign
that gets that back into

00:18:24.976 --> 00:18:27.180
the forefront of voter's minds, so I think

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.276
that's definitely a contender.

00:18:29.276 --> 00:18:31.430
And then films that
have yet to be released,

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:33.080
we just don't know what
December's gonna bring yet,

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:35.183
what the quality of those
movies are gonna be,

00:18:36.153 --> 00:18:37.420
and I haven't seen Ford v Ferrari yet --

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:40.800
- Ford Ferrari is fantastic.
I've seen it twice

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:42.650
and it its absolutely best picture --

00:18:43.887 --> 00:18:46.361
- Good to know where I
stand and where you stand.

00:18:46.361 --> 00:18:47.720
(laughing)

00:18:47.720 --> 00:18:50.190
- I mean Mark, the other film
that we're not talking about

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:53.440
that is absolutely a best
picture contender, and to me

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.330
where I sit right now
on this day taping this,

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:59.660
my favorite movie of the
year is Marriage Story,

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:01.370
and that is also a Netflix film.

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:04.240
And that's like the Kramer vs.
Kramer of the 21st Century.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.050
It's a brilliant, masterful film,

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.500
written and directed by Noah Baumbach.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:11.710
You also have 1917,
directed by Sam Mendes,

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:13.240
that's not opening until December,

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:15.710
they're not screening it until the weekend

00:19:18.425 --> 00:19:19.619
of November 23rd and 24th,
and the people are really

00:19:19.619 --> 00:19:21.170
gonna see how this one
take experiment worked.

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:23.375
- [ Angie] And don't forget Cats!

00:19:23.375 --> 00:19:26.170
- Well Cats could go either
way, I'm hoping it goes

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.823
the good way, but that
trailer was really awkward.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.737
- Taylor Swift fans loved it.

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:35.540
- I think that it's good
that we don't know exactly

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:37.130
for sure yeah this is the movie.

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:38.570
- Yeah some people are speculating,

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.500
I don't necessarily agree with
this, but that it could be

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:42.510
a Tarantino versus Scorsese,

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:45.760
because Once Upon a Time In
Hollywood certainly deserves

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:46.593
consideration.

00:19:46.593 --> 00:19:47.698
- Absolutely, yeah that's another film.

00:19:47.698 --> 00:19:48.653
- The other thing is, it's just so early.

00:19:48.653 --> 00:19:51.490
I don't think that necessarily
this time last year

00:19:51.490 --> 00:19:53.440
people would have been
like, Green Book, obviously

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.450
the winner. So a lot of things can happen,

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:57.770
like I think we can make
our guesses about which ones

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.513
we think are worthy, and
which ones we think are likely

00:19:59.513 --> 00:20:02.710
given what we know about what
has done well in the past,

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:03.543
but we don't know for sure.

00:20:03.543 --> 00:20:04.490
I will say that Netflix,

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:07.590
I feel like they've been
trying to establish themselves

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:09.980
as a major award season
player for a while.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:12.290
It started years ago
with Beasts of No Nation,

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:14.880
which went nowhere, and it
seems like they are getting

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:17.410
closer and closer, and The
Irishman just seems like

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:18.490
a really good shot.

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:21.100
You've got a director that
everyone loves and has

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:25.180
unanimously agreed is a master filmmaker.

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:28.370
You've got him playing
a different variation on

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:31.330
a theme that we have always
liked from him in the past.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:34.950
You've got these three actors
that are very well respected

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.779
and have been for a long time.

00:20:36.779 --> 00:20:40.140
- The other thing, you touched
on this about the freedom

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.900
of working with Netflix.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:46.110
The Irishman was originally
set up at Paramount.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.870
When the budget started to
creep up past 100 million,

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.777
Paramount, short story, just said,

00:20:50.777 --> 00:20:53.710
thanks, no thanks,
we're gonna let this go,

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:55.180
feel free to take it elsewhere.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.880
Well, when the producer
is like Jane Rosenthal's

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:00.450
longtime producing partner Robert De Niro,

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:02.120
they shopped it around.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.960
Nobody was actually biting
at their heels to take on

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:07.040
The Irishman because the
budget kept creeping up,

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.570
they did not think that
it was a sure thing

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:12.300
or a slam dunk despite
the fact that you have

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:15.910
Pesci and De Niro and Al
Pacino and Harvey Keitel,

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.690
and all these longtime
players, despite the fact that

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:21.190
this is gonna be the
ninth film that Scorsese

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:23.670
and De Niro have made
together, it's a mob movie,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:25.303
which everyone loves a mob movie.

00:21:25.303 --> 00:21:27.484
Then Netflix says, actually hey,

00:21:27.484 --> 00:21:30.610
we'll take it, here's a blank
check, go make your movie,

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:32.930
you have complete creative control.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:36.108
The movie cost 150
million dollars to make,

00:21:36.108 --> 00:21:39.420
and your probably gonna
get a whole lot of people

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.510
subscribing to Netflix
just to watch The Irishman

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:44.185
if they don't see it in theaters.

00:21:44.185 --> 00:21:46.150
- Netflix's business model is different,

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:50.110
so the calculations they're
making when they try to decide

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:52.610
how much money am I going
to give to this filmmaker,

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:55.860
is not gonna be exactly
the same calculations that

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:58.940
Warner Brothers or Disney or
Sony or someone else is making.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:00.610
- [Mark] I think Michael
Phillips said it really well

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:03.120
from the Chicago Tribune,
he said this movie "it moves

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:04.531
forward and backward and
sideways, getting more

00:22:04.531 --> 00:22:07.220
unpredictable and compelling as it goes".

00:22:07.220 --> 00:22:09.130
It's a worthy theater experience in mind.

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:10.590
It's actually playing in
a limited run of theaters

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:11.423
right now.

00:22:11.423 --> 00:22:13.800
It's also gonna be
streaming for Thanksgiving.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.010
So here's the big
question, here's the last

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:17.135
question of the day.

00:22:17.135 --> 00:22:19.345
When do you put on The Irishman during

00:22:19.345 --> 00:22:21.600
the Thanksgiving weekend?

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.670
Is it what you're watching
instead of football

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:24.790
during the day?

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:27.110
Do you have the meal and
then put it on Thanksgiving

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:27.943
night?

00:22:27.943 --> 00:22:30.104
There's a risk of falling
asleep, three and a half hours.

00:22:30.104 --> 00:22:32.340
- [Angie] I feel like after a
Thanksgiving meal is actually

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:33.480
a terrible time to watch
because the beginning is

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:35.710
the slowest part.
- The turkey coma.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:37.790
- Everyone's already full.

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:41.680
You're barely awake as it
is. You put on The Irishman,

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:43.150
you're like, it's a three
and a half hour movie,

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:44.910
it starts our very slow
and then it becomes,

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:49.410
like a poetic meditation
on mortality and aging,

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:51.270
and people are gonna be like "Um, no."

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:52.103
(laughing)

00:22:52.103 --> 00:22:53.992
- I disagree. I think right
after Thanksgiving dinner

00:22:53.992 --> 00:22:56.850
is the best time to watch
The Irishman because you

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.070
know what? You are gonna
be full, yeah you might be

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:01.190
a little tired, but you're
really not gonna wanna

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:03.970
talk to anybody after
sitting at the dinner table

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:05.850
for a few hours during
Thanksgiving dinner.

00:23:05.850 --> 00:23:07.630
You're gonna just wanna
just chill out and veg out

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.433
and watch something on TV --

00:23:09.433 --> 00:23:10.266
- Will Marriage Story
be out at that point?

00:23:10.266 --> 00:23:11.099
- No it won't. Not on Netflix.

00:23:11.099 --> 00:23:12.170
- Oh well that's too bad.
That would be a great

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:13.003
family movie.

00:23:13.003 --> 00:23:13.836
(laughs)

00:23:13.836 --> 00:23:14.669
- Can you imagine?

00:23:14.669 --> 00:23:16.160
But no, The Irishman is
gonna be perfect because

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:19.030
it'll be like, oh yeah Scorsese mob movie,

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.749
This is perfect and I don't
have to talk to anybody else

00:23:21.749 --> 00:23:23.940
for three and a half hours, boom!

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:25.560
- I thought of the best time to watch it,

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:26.710
the next morning!

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:27.742
- Oh forget it! It's not a morning movie!

00:23:27.742 --> 00:23:29.445
- Yes it is!

00:23:29.445 --> 00:23:30.688
- No way!

00:23:30.688 --> 00:23:31.521
- It's a total Black Friday movie.

00:23:31.521 --> 00:23:32.701
Let everybody else go out and get --

00:23:32.701 --> 00:23:33.890
- Yeah exactly, everyone's shopping,

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.610
you're combing through
your fridge for leftovers,

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.510
you're also with your family,
so you guys need something

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:40.730
to do together but now
everyone's awake, you've had

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:41.640
your coffee, your --

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.820
- Or at night you start
watching it and you hit pause

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.500
after two hours then finish
watching the rest of it

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.562
the next day.

00:23:47.562 --> 00:23:48.395
- No!

00:23:48.395 --> 00:23:50.210
- What I was gonna do is
just wait until Cyber Monday,

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:51.910
that way we can have a Netflix window open

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:53.520
then I can just do all
my Christmas shopping --

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:54.760
(laughing)

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:56.430
I'll just have everything in
front of me, in one easy thing.

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:57.774
- No way mister.

00:23:57.774 --> 00:23:58.650
- As Martin Scorsese
intended it to be seen.

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:00.963
- That's right (laughs)

00:24:00.963 --> 00:24:04.880
(all talking and laughing)

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:08.285
I'm gonna go to my movie
theater right now, look at that.

00:24:08.285 --> 00:24:09.118
(laughing)

00:24:09.118 --> 00:24:10.610
Thank you so much Scott Mantz, Angie Han.

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:12.230
Thanks for joining me on
this conversation with

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:13.260
The Irishman.

